    {
      "id": "sva",
      "title": "Subject–Verb Agreement",
      "icon": "🧩",
      "description": "The verb must match the subject in number. Singular subject → singular verb. Plural subject → plural verb.",
      "rules": [
        "A number of + plural noun → plural verb. The number of + plural noun → singular verb.",
        "One of + plural noun → singular verb.",
        "Each / Every / Either / Neither → singular verb.",
        "Neither...nor / Either...or / Not only...but also → verb agrees with the NEARER subject.",
        "Along with / together with / as well as / besides / in addition to → verb agrees with the FIRST subject.",
        "Two nouns joined by 'and' referring to one idea → singular. Referring to two things → plural.",
        "Book titles, country names, distances, sums of money → singular.",
        "Many a + singular noun → singular verb. More than one + singular noun → singular verb.",
        "The poor / the rich / the unemployed → plural. Cattle / police / gentry / people → plural.",
        "News / mathematics / physics / economics / politics → singular.",
        "A series of → singular. A variety of → plural. The variety of → singular.",
        "Half of / per cent of / a lot of → depends on the noun after 'of'.",
        "It is I who / It is you who → verb agrees with the pronoun after 'who'."
      ],
      "cheatCodes": [
        "Find the REAL subject first. Ignore phrases between subject and verb.",
        "'Of' phrases: look at the noun BEFORE 'of', not after.",
        "'And' usually = plural, unless it's one idea (rice and curry, law and order).",
        "Titles and amounts = singular even if they look plural.",
        "'Either/Neither' alone = singular. 'Either...or/Neither...nor' = nearer subject."
      ],
      "questions": [
