      "questions": [
        {"q":"PASSAGE: On August 3, 1492, Christopher Columbus set sail from Palos, Spain, with less than a hundred crew members to discover a new route to Asia. After spending a difficult time at sea, the party sighted land early on the morning of October 12, 1492. They set foot on an island in the Bahamas which they named Al Salvador. Columbus presumed that the indigenous people were Native Indians as he was under the mistaken belief that he had set foot on Indian soil. Probably some 10 million American Indians were natives to the land before the large-scale inhabitation by Europeans and subsequent annihilation of Native Americans started. However, it took more than a hundred years after Columbus discovered America for the Europeans to finally take the momentous decision to make the New World their home. The Native Americans actually welcomed the pale-skinned visitors primarily out of curiosity. They were fascinated by the steel knives and swords, fire spewing cannons, brass and copper utensils. Eventually, cultural differences erupted. The natives could not stomach the arrogance of the newcomers and the scant respect they paid to nature. The European settlers viewed every resource — plants, animals, and people — as something to be commercially exploited. The native Indians were vastly outnumbered in the wars that ensued. The resistance they put up never proved enough. The nomadic lifestyle of the Indians, the relatively unsophisticated weapons at their disposal, the unwillingness of some of their own people to defend themselves, and the diseases of the white men — all contributed to the virtual elimination of their race. Some of the diseases brought by Europeans that decimated the natives were: small pox, plague, measles, cholera, typhoid, and malaria. These deadly diseases, to which most natives had developed no resistance, devastated many tribes between 1775 and 1850. America was named after an Italian navigator, Amerigo Vespucci. QUESTION: The primary purpose of the passage is to:","options":["Disprove the notion that America was named after Columbus","Provide a snapshot of the discovery of America and the early years of settlements","Explain how the Europeans eliminated the Native Americans in their own land","Discuss how the process of colonization of America started"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The passage covers discovery + early settlements + native relations."},
        {"q":"From the passage we can infer that in comparison to the Europeans, Native Americans were:","options":["Careless about their environment","A very unhealthy lot","More respectful of nature","Ignorant about sanitation"],"answer":2,"explanation":"'scant respect they paid to nature' — Europeans had scant respect. So natives respected nature more."},
        {"q":"What can be inferred from the third paragraph?","options":["The native Americans did not have any weapons with which to defend themselves.","The native Americans probably attached a lot of importance to and respected nature.","The native Americans did not know how to use natural resources.","The early settlers became arrogant as they could commercially exploit resources."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Europeans were criticized for 'scant respect to nature', so natives valued nature."},
